    "seo": {
      "title": "Archaeological Site Conservation in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore archaeological conservation in Iran through Persepolis, Bam, and the principles governing excavation, maintenance, authenticity, and setting."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "protecting-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Protecting evidence, including what remains underground",
        "content": "An archaeological site is more than a collection of objects or impressive walls. Its value also lies in the relationships among remains, their original locations, and the records produced when they are investigated. The archaeological heritage charter adopted by the International Council on Monuments and Sites, or ICOMOS, treats protection as a continuing responsibility involving researchers, public authorities, other specialists, and communities. [s1](https://icomosjapan.org/static/homepage/charter/charter1990.pdf)\n\nThis approach makes excavation a consequential decision. The charter recommends leaving part of an investigated site undisturbed for future research and making excavation reports available to the scholarly community. Its conservation principle is preservation in situ: keeping heritage in its original context. It also cautions against exposing remains when their subsequent maintenance cannot be assured. These are professional standards, rather than evidence that every Iranian excavation follows them. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persepolis",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persepolis: protecting ruins and their surroundings",
        "content": "At Persepolis, the Achaemenid royal complex in southwestern Iran, conservation concerns both surviving monuments and the land around them. UNESCO identifies agricultural expansion, construction, industrial development, and the growth of nearby settlements as pressures on the property and its buffer zone. A buffer zone helps protect the surroundings that contribute to a heritage property’s significance; it is not simply unused land awaiting development. [s2](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/114/)\n\nUNESCO’s statement describes restoration using traditional materials and technology while respecting the ensemble’s authenticity. It identifies the Persepolis Research Base as responsible for investigation, conservation, restoration, organization, and presentation. Its management requirements also call for monitoring pollutants, weathering, and other environmental factors. These requirements establish what needs sustained attention; the listing statement alone does not demonstrate that every monitoring measure is operating effectively today. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "bam",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Bam: earthen architecture within an oasis",
        "content": "Bam presents a different conservation problem. Its fortified settlement uses layered earth and sun-dried mud brick, while the surrounding oasis depends historically on qanats, underground channels that convey water. UNESCO treats the settlement, hydraulic systems, agricultural land, and their relationships as a cultural landscape. Conserving the citadel therefore cannot be separated completely from conserving its setting. [s3](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1208/)\n\nThe 2003 earthquake damaged the citadel, including sections of its defensive walls and governor’s quarters. UNESCO’s conservation statement emphasizes original materials, traditional techniques, and the reliability of documentary and field evidence when considering restoration or partial reconstruction. The distinction matters: a convincing new surface does not by itself establish historical accuracy. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-conservation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to assess conservation claims",
        "content": "These examples suggest useful questions when reading about an Iranian archaeological project. What evidence supports the intervention? How will exposed remains be maintained? Does protection extend to the surrounding landscape? Are published management requirements being mistaken for completed work? The answers help distinguish a documented conservation approach from an attractive presentation of ruins. Conservation succeeds over time through continued care, research, and protection of context. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
